1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,189
Module 14 saves its flagship demo for last: an actual Linux kernel, booted from inside a browser tab, with a filesystem the model genuinely shares.

2
00:00:06,189 --> 00:00:08,000
Let's turn it on and see what it really is.

3
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:08,794
It's off by default.

4
00:00:08,794 --> 00:00:11,095
In Settings, there's a toggle for the real-Linux terminal.

5
00:00:11,095 --> 00:00:18,000
Flip it on, and from then on run_command routes there instead of the coreutils shell shim we saw back in episode fifty-seven — the model is now talking to an actual guest OS.

6
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:23,592
What's actually booting: Buildroot and BusyBox, running under v86 — an open-source x86-to-WebAssembly emulator — driven over its serial console.

7
00:00:23,592 --> 00:00:26,388
That means real uname, real busybox grep, real arbitrary guest programs.

8
00:00:26,388 --> 00:00:30,000
Not a JavaScript re-implementation of Unix — genuine Linux, emulated down to the instruction.

9
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:32,063
Why v86 and not the faster alternative, CheerpX?

10
00:00:32,063 --> 00:00:32,492
Licensing.

11
00:00:32,492 --> 00:00:34,555
v86 is BSD-licensed and free for commercial use.

12
00:00:34,555 --> 00:00:37,691
CheerpX is proprietary and would mean billing every user of this feature.

13
00:00:37,691 --> 00:00:41,000
Slower but free beats fast but metered, for a tool meant to ship to everyone.

14
00:00:41,000 --> 00:00:43,309
The part worth lingering on: the 9p filesystem bridge.

15
00:00:43,309 --> 00:00:47,586
The guest mounts the shared tree at /mnt, and the model's OPFS workspace lives under /mnt/workspace.

16
00:00:47,586 --> 00:00:54,000
So a file the file tools wrote and a file a Linux command creates aren't synced copies — they're literally the same file, seen from two sides at once.

17
00:00:54,000 --> 00:01:01,053
State the costs plainly: about a ten megabyte one-time kernel download, a few seconds to boot, and emulated execution that's slower than native.

18
00:01:01,053 --> 00:01:06,000
It's headless and serial-only — there's no GUI here, no windows, just a terminal talking to a kernel.

19
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:09,964
Gotcha: this is a genuinely experimental, look-what's-possible demo, not a daily driver.

20
00:01:09,964 --> 00:01:16,000
Expect roughly a forty second boot, and a thirty second timeout on each command — plan your demos, and your expectations, around that.

21
00:01:16,000 --> 00:01:22,000
Module fifteen turns to something much more precise: reaching back into a conversation and rewinding it to an earlier point.

